From: U2W build
Subject: [PATCH] aacdec: an input byte count passed as output size means "unknown", for
 the CarPlay AAC-ELD contract only

0.1.5 added
  if (timeDataSize_extern < numChannels*frameSize) ErrorStatus = AAC_DEC_OUTPUT_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL;
0.1.4, the vendor library AppleCarPlay was built against (byte-identical in every Carlinkit
firmware 2023-2025), ignored timeDataSize and decoded the frame in place.

Measured in the car (2026-10-19, VW MIB2G, instrumented build): on its AAC-ELD call-audio path
(AOT 39, 16 kHz, mono, frameSize 480, aacSamplesPerFrame 480, no SBR) AppleCarPlay passes as
timeDataSize the input byte count it just handed to aacDecoder_Fill (2, 25, 45, 74, 117,
148..181: the packet lengths).  0.1.6 rejected every frame with 0x200c and calls were silent.

The vendor allocation contract: the CarPlay SDK's AAC-ELD converter
(APSAudioConverterStub.c, _AudioConverterFillComplexBufferAACELDDecode) returns kSizeErr unless
*ioOutputDataPacketSize >= kAudioSamplesPerPacket_AAC_ELD (480), so it only decodes into a
buffer of at least 480 frames; AudioConverterNew refuses channel mixing, so a frame has the
stream's channel count, which the AudioSpecificConfig signals (mono or stereo for CarPlay).

aacDecoder_Fill records the input byte count per decoder handle.  The check is skipped, and the
frame written as 0.1.4 did (numChannels*frameSize samples), only when ALL hold:
  - timeDataSize == the last aacDecoder_Fill byte count;
  - streamInfo.aot == AOT_ER_AAC_ELD;
  - 0 < frameSize <= 480 (so ELD 512, and ELD with dual-rate SBR (960), keep the check);
  - 0 < numChannels <= channelConfig <= 2 (no upmix beyond the configured channel count).
The write is then at most 480*numChannels samples: inside the SDK's buffer.  Logged once per
handle.  Every other stream, frame length or value keeps the 0.1.6 check, so a coincident
length on AAC-LC or a real undersized buffer is still rejected (patch 010 bounds the clear).

Residual: the decoder cannot see the allocation.  An ELD caller outside that contract that
passes its fill length as timeDataSize gets a 480-frame write whatever its buffer holds.
fdk-aac/tests/run.sh checks this with guard-page output buffers.

--- a/libAACdec/src/aacdecoder_lib.cpp
+++ b/libAACdec/src/aacdecoder_lib.cpp
@@ -658,6 +658,8 @@
     goto bail;
   }
   aacDec->hInput = pIn;
+  aacDec->u2wLastFillBytes = -1;
+  aacDec->u2wFillSizeLogged = 0;
 
   aacDec->nrOfLayers = nrOfLayers;
 
@@ -717,6 +719,9 @@
      available layer                                                           */
   INT layer      = 0;
   INT nrOfLayers = self->nrOfLayers;
+
+  /* U2W: remember the input byte count; see the output-buffer check in aacDecoder_DecodeFrame. */
+  self->u2wLastFillBytes = (nrOfLayers > 0) ? (INT)bufferSize[0] : -1;
 
   {
     for (layer = 0; layer < nrOfLayers; layer++){
@@ -1052,9 +1057,39 @@
     /* Update Statistics */
     aacDecoder_UpdateBitStreamCounters(&self->streamInfo, hBs, nBits, ErrorStatus);
 
-    /* Check whether external output buffer is large enough. */
-    if (timeDataSize_extern < self->streamInfo.numChannels*self->streamInfo.frameSize) {
-      ErrorStatus = AAC_DEC_OUTPUT_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL;
+    /* Check whether external output buffer is large enough.
+       U2W: AppleCarPlay's AAC-ELD (call audio) path passes the input byte count it just gave
+       aacDecoder_Fill as timeDataSize (measured in the car 2026-10-19: 2..181, always the
+       packet length; AOT 39, 16 kHz, mono, frame 480, no SBR).  That is no output size:
+       fdk-aac 0.1.4, the vendor library, ignored the argument and wrote the frame, and the
+       CarPlay SDK only decodes AAC-ELD into a buffer of at least kAudioSamplesPerPacket_AAC_ELD
+       (480) frames of the stream's channel count.  Only that contract skips the check: the
+       value is the last fill length, the stream is ER AAC-ELD, the decoded frame is at most
+       480 samples per channel and has no more channels than the stream configuration (mono
+       or stereo).  The write is then numChannels*frameSize <= 480*channels samples, inside
+       the SDK's buffer.  Every other stream, frame length or value keeps the check. */
+    {
+      const INT u2wFrameSamples = self->streamInfo.numChannels*self->streamInfo.frameSize;
+      const int u2wEldContract =
+           (timeDataSize_extern == self->u2wLastFillBytes)
+        && (self->streamInfo.aot == AOT_ER_AAC_ELD)
+        && (self->streamInfo.frameSize > 0)
+        && (self->streamInfo.frameSize <= 480)
+        && (self->streamInfo.numChannels > 0)
+        && (self->streamInfo.numChannels <= self->streamInfo.channelConfig)
+        && (self->streamInfo.channelConfig <= 2);
+
+      if (u2wEldContract) {
+        if (!self->u2wFillSizeLogged) {
+          self->u2wFillSizeLogged = 1;
+          FDKprintfErr("fdk-aac: timeDataSize %d is the input byte count, not an output size; "
+                       "writing one AAC-ELD frame (%d samples) as fdk-aac 0.1.4 did\n",
+                       timeDataSize_extern, u2wFrameSamples);
+        }
+      }
+      else if (timeDataSize_extern < u2wFrameSamples) {
+        ErrorStatus = AAC_DEC_OUTPUT_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL;
+      }
     }
 
     /* Update external output buffer. */
--- a/libAACdec/src/aacdecoder.h
+++ b/libAACdec/src/aacdecoder.h
@@ -227,6 +227,8 @@
   UINT         extGainDelay;                         /*!< Delay that must be accounted for extGain. */
 
   INT_PCM      pcmOutputBuffer[(8)*(2048)];
+  INT          u2wLastFillBytes;   /* U2W: input bytes of the last aacDecoder_Fill (-1: none) */
+  INT          u2wFillSizeLogged;
 
 };
 
